---
import SiteLayout from "../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import FlavorTabs from "../../../components/FlavorTabs.astro";
import ModuleHero from "../../../components/module/ModuleHero.astro";
import ModuleScenario from "../../../components/module/ModuleScenario.astro";
import ModuleFlowDiagram from "../../../components/module/ModuleFlowDiagram.astro";
import TraceWaterfallDemo from "../../../components/module/demos/TraceWaterfallDemo.astro";
import AgentTraceTreeDemo from "../../../components/module/demos/AgentTraceTreeDemo.astro";
import ModuleFooterNav from "../../../components/module/ModuleFooterNav.astro";
import ModuleRecap from "../../../components/module/ModuleRecap.astro";
import ModuleQuiz from "../../../components/module/ModuleQuiz.astro";
import { flavor as gcp } from "../../../data/flavors/gcp";
import { flavor as aws } from "../../../data/flavors/aws";
import { flavor as azure } from "../../../data/flavors/azure";
import { flavor as oss } from "../../../data/flavors/oss";
import { MODULES } from "../../../data/modules";

const moduleId = "55-observability";
const entries = {
  gcp: gcp[moduleId],
  aws: aws[moduleId],
  azure: azure[moduleId],
  oss: oss[moduleId],
};
const moduleData = MODULES.find((m) => m.id === moduleId)!;
---

<SiteLayout title="55 · Observability for LLMs" description={moduleData.summary}>
  <article>
    <ModuleHero moduleId={moduleId}>
      <p>
        Module 45 told you your pipeline is good on average, on a golden set you built ahead of time. Observability
        answers the harder question: what happened on <em>this specific request</em>, right now, in production — and
        it builds the event log every later module (evaluation regressions, security audits, cost analysis) ends up
        reading from.
      </p>
    </ModuleHero>

    <ModuleScenario moduleId={moduleId} />

    <ModuleFlowDiagram moduleId={moduleId} />

    <h2>Traces and spans for RAG</h2>
    <p>A single user question touches four stages — logged separately, they're nearly impossible to reconstruct.</p>
    <div class="concept-flow" role="list" aria-label="RAG request stages">
      <span class="chip chip--accent" role="listitem">EMBED QUERY</span>
      <span class="concept-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      <span class="chip" role="listitem">RETRIEVE CHUNKS</span>
      <span class="concept-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      <span class="chip" role="listitem">ASSEMBLE PROMPT</span>
      <span class="concept-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      <span class="chip" role="listitem">CALL MODEL</span>
    </div>
    <p>
      Wrap the whole request in one <strong>trace</strong>, with each stage as a nested <strong>span</strong>. When a
      user reports a bad answer, open one trace and see exactly which stage went wrong — instead of grepping four
      logs and hoping the timestamps line up.
    </p>

    <TraceWaterfallDemo />

    <h2>Tracing an agent loop: a tree, not a waterfall</h2>
    <p class="concept-comparison-lede">Trace shape follows call shape:</p>
    <div class="concept-comparison" role="list">
      <div class="concept-comparison-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="chip chip--accent">WATERFALL</span>
        <p>A RAG call is linear — embed, retrieve, assemble, generate — one bar per stage.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="concept-comparison-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="chip">TREE</span>
        <p>Module 85's agent loop repeats plan/act/observe, and a tool call can trigger nested calls — each iteration is a span with its own child spans.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      Flattening an agent trace back into a waterfall loses exactly the information you need most — which iteration,
      how many levels deep, is where things went wrong. A RAG trace only has to answer "which of these four stages
      was slow or wrong"; an agent trace needs an iteration index and a parent-span link on every tool-call span.
    </p>

    <AgentTraceTreeDemo />

    <h2>Token accounting per request</h2>
    <p>
      Attach input, output, and cached token counts as attributes on the generation span, per request. This looks
      like overkill until Module 75, where the FinOps unit-economics work is a straight SQL join against this exact
      data — without it, cost analysis degenerates into "the monthly bill went up, no idea why."
    </p>

    <h2>Latency budgets: TTFT vs. total latency</h2>
    <p class="concept-comparison-lede">Two different latency numbers, both worth budgeting separately:</p>
    <div class="concept-comparison" role="list">
      <div class="concept-comparison-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="chip chip--accent">TTFT</span>
        <p>Time-to-first-token — how long a user waits before anything appears, the number that makes a streaming response feel instant or sluggish.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="concept-comparison-item" role="listitem">
        <span class="chip">TOTAL LATENCY</span>
        <p>How long the full answer takes — a model can have great TTFT and mediocre total latency, or the reverse.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>Track both: a chat UI cares more about TTFT, a batch job cares only about total latency.</p>

    <h2>The event log as the analytical backbone</h2>
    <p>
      Once every request is a trace with token counts, latency, and outcome, that trace store stops being "just
      logs" and becomes the platform's central table: Module 45's regression evals sample production traffic from
      it, Module 65's security review searches it for suspicious patterns, Module 75's cost analysis joins directly
      against it. Instrument this once, well, and the rest of the advanced curriculum runs without re-plumbing
      logging every time.
    </p>

    <h2>Try it in your flavor</h2>
    <FlavorTabs moduleId={moduleId} entries={entries} />

    <h2>Pitfalls</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Logging only the final response text — you lose the ability to tell whether retrieval or generation caused a bad answer.</li>
      <li>Skipping token-count attributes because "we'll add cost tracking later" — Module 75 needs this data from day one, not retroactively.</li>
      <li>Optimizing only for total latency and ignoring TTFT, which is what users actually perceive as "slow" in a streaming UI.</li>
      <li>Treating traces as a debugging-only tool instead of the shared data source that evaluation, security, and cost modules all depend on.</li>
      <li>Flattening an agent's tree-shaped trace back into a single waterfall — you lose which loop iteration and which nested tool call actually failed.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
  <ModuleQuiz moduleId={moduleId} />
  <ModuleRecap moduleId={moduleId} />
  <ModuleFooterNav moduleId={moduleId} />
</SiteLayout>
